## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| **Thread.Abort doesn't exist** — no fallback "force kill" | Design `Handle()` to check `_cts.Token` periodically if it does long work |
| **Lost signals** (original bug) | Drain the queue in a `while` loop after each wake, not just one item |
| **CancellationTokenSource leak** | Always `Dispose()` it — it holds timer/callback resources |
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | If `Handle()` runs for minutes, it won't stop until the next iteration. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long operations |
| **WaitHandle.WaitAny ordering** | Put `token.WaitHandle` last if you want to process remaining items before exit, or first to prioritize shutdown |

---

## Recommended Approach

Use the **Channel-based version** (first example). It eliminates manual locking, the signal race condition, and aligns with modern .NET idioms. `Channel<T>` is purpose-built for producer/consumer scenarios and is allocation-efficient.
